      <!-- Section 1: Thermodynamics -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          1. The Thermodynamics of Human Energy Balance
        </h2>
        <p>
          Human weight regulation is fundamentally governed by the <strong>First Law of Thermodynamics</strong>: energy within a closed biological system cannot be created or destroyed, only converted from one chemical state to another. In physiological terms:
        </p>
        
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto my-4 border border-zinc-800">
          <code>Rate of Energy Storage (ΔFat + ΔMuscle) = Energy Ingested (ME) - Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)</code>
        </div>

        <p>
          While the basic equation is linear, physiological energy expenditure is dynamic and adaptive. When an individual enters a sustained caloric deficit, the human body engages in <strong>adaptive thermogenesis</strong>—an evolutionary survival mechanism that downregulates resting energy expenditure, reduces spontaneous non-exercise movement (NEAT), and enhances mitochondrial energy coupling efficiency to conserve adipose stores.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: The Four Pillars of TDEE -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          2. The Four Fundamental Components of Total Daily Energy Expenditure
        </h2>
        <p>
          Your daily caloric expenditure is not a single static number; it is the mathematical sum of four distinct metabolic components:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
          
          <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-bold text-emerald-500 text-base">Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)</span>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-zinc-500">60% – 70%</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-normal">
              The baseline energy required to sustain life in a post-absorptive state at thermoneutral temperature (ion pumping in kidneys, hepatic protein synthesis, cardiac pumping, and brain glucose oxidation).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-bold text-cyan-500 text-base">NEAT (Non-Exercise Activity)</span>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-zinc-500">15% – 20%</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-normal">
              Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis encompasses all spontaneous physical activity: standing, walking, fidgeting, maintaining posture, and typing. It is the single most variable component across individuals.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
              <span class="font-bold text-amber-500 text-base">Thermic Effect of Food (TEF)</span>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-zinc-500">8% – 10%</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-normal">
              The metabolic cost of digesting, absorbing, and assimilating nutrients. Protein has the highest TEF (20%–30% of ingested calories burned), followed by carbohydrates (5%–10%) and fats (0%–3%).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
