      <!-- Section 3: Protein Leverage & Satiety -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          3. The Protein Leverage Hypothesis & Satiety Signaling
        </h2>
        <p>
          Formulated by evolutionary biologists David Raubenheimer and Stephen Simpson, the <strong>Protein Leverage Hypothesis</strong> posits that human appetite regulation prioritizes protein intake above all other macronutrients. The central nervous system drives food consumption until target protein requirements are met. When diets are diluted with refined fats and carbohydrates, individuals overconsume total energy to satisfy absolute amino acid needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Maintaining high protein intake (1.8g to 2.4g per kg of body weight) during a caloric deficit activates three distinct satiety cascades:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            <strong>Ghrelin Suppression:</strong> High protein downregulates postprandial ghrelin (the primary hunger hormone secreted by the stomach fundus) significantly longer than carbohydrates or lipids.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Incretin Stimulation (GLP-1 & PYY):</strong> Protein ingestion stimulates L-cells in the distal ileum to release Glucagon-Like Peptide-1 (GLP-1) and Peptide YY, delaying gastric emptying and signaling fullness to the hypothalamus.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Muscle Protein Synthesis (MPS) Spared:</strong> Leucine-rich protein triggers the mTORC1 pathway, protecting lean muscle tissue from gluconeogenic breakdown during caloric restriction.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: The 69 kcal/kg Adipose Limit -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          4. The Mathematical Limit of Fat Loss: The 69 kcal/kg Adipose Constraint
        </h2>
        <p>
          A landmark study by Seymour Alpert (2005, <em>Journal of Theoretical Biology</em>) demonstrated that there is a strict physiological ceiling on the rate at which human adipose tissue can liberate fatty acids into circulation for beta-oxidation. The maximum energy transfer rate is mathematically constrained to:
        </p>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-100 font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto my-4 border border-zinc-800">
          <code>Max Adipose Energy Transfer = 69.3 ± 12.8 kcal per kg of body fat per day</code>
        </div>

        <p>
          If an individual creates a daily caloric deficit larger than their total adipose capacity (e.g. an individual with 10 kg of fat attempting an 800 kcal deficit when their maximum transfer is 10 &times; 69 = 690 kcal/day), the metabolic shortfall is forcefully made up through the catabolism of functional lean muscle tissue and organ proteins via hepatic gluconeogenesis.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Neuro-Metabolic Brain Glucose -->
      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          5. Neuro-Metabolic Demands: Brain Glucose Perfusion & Mental Focus
        </h2>
        <p>
          While skeletal muscle can readily switch between lipid oxidation, glycogenolysis, and circulating fatty acids, the human brain requires continuous metabolic perfusion. Under standard dietary conditions, the central nervous system consumes approximately <strong>120 grams of glucose per day</strong> (representing ~20% of total Basal Metabolic Rate).
        </p>
        <p>
          During aggressive caloric restriction, insufficient carbohydrate availability or erratic blood sugar swings can precipitate acute mental fatigue, impaired executive function, and reduced working memory capacity. Maintaining a balanced macronutrient distribution or allowing 2–3 weeks of hepatic keto-adaptation ensures sustained cognitive focus during weight loss.
        </p>
      </section>

