            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white cursor-pointer select-none text-sm sm:text-base">
              <span>How much energy does the human brain consume daily?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" /></svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 pt-3">
              The human brain comprises ~2% of body mass but consumes ~20% of your resting Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)—approximately 300 to 400 kcal per day (equivalent to ~100-130 grams of pure glucose) for continuous neuronal ion transport.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white cursor-pointer select-none text-sm sm:text-base">
              <span>How much daily protein do I need while cutting calories?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" /></svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 pt-3">
              Clinical guidelines recommend 1.8 to 2.4 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight (0.8 to 1.1 g/lb). High protein leverages the Thermic Effect of Food (TEF burns 20-30% of protein calories), spares lean muscle, and boosts satiety hormones like GLP-1 and PYY.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white cursor-pointer select-none text-sm sm:text-base">
              <span>What is metabolic adaptation (starvation mode)?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" /></svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 pt-3">
              Adaptive thermogenesis is an evolutionary mechanism where your body downregulates non-exercise activity (NEAT), decreases resting heart rate, and lowers leptin and thyroid hormones (T3). To prevent severe metabolic adaptation, avoid deficits over 25% and take structured diet breaks.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white cursor-pointer select-none text-sm sm:text-base">
              <span>How does the US Navy Body Fat formula estimate body fat?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" /></svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 pt-3">
              The US Navy formula uses logarithmic circumference ratios of the neck, waist (at navel), and hips (for females) against standing height. It provides a reliable estimate with a 3-4% margin of error compared to DEXA scans.
            </p>
          </details>

          <details class="group bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 rounded-2xl p-4 transition-all [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white cursor-pointer select-none text-sm sm:text-base">
              <span>Should I eat back the calories burned during exercise?</span>
              <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M19.5 8.25l-7.5 7.5-7.5-7.5" /></svg>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs sm:text-sm text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 pt-3">
              In most cases, no. Fitness trackers overestimate cardio expenditure by 20% to 40%. Because TDEE calculations already account for your weekly workout frequency via the activity multiplier, eating back tracked exercise calories usually erases your caloric deficit.
            </p>
          </details>

        </div>
      </section>

